# examples/.gitignore
#
# WHAT THIS REPO IS: a corpus of Y programs, each in its own self-contained
# folder, each with a README saying what it is, how to build it, how to run
# it and what to look at. That is the deliverable.
#
# WHAT IS NOT: the acceptance gate, the property-check harnesses, the defect
# injectors, the pty capture scripts and the fixture generators. Those exist
# to *verify* the corpus and were never part of it, so none of them is
# tracked. They are plain Python and shell — no Y, because a verification
# harness is not an example and does not belong in the examples tree.
#
# The rule is a DENY list, not an allow list, and that is deliberate. An
# allow list ("track *.y, *.md, *.txt") silently drops any data fixture whose
# name I failed to anticipate, and a missing fixture breaks an example that
# otherwise looks perfect. A deny list drops only what is named below, so
# anything unforeseen stays tracked and gets noticed.
#
# Nothing here affects the working tree. The harnesses remain on disk and the
# campaign still gates with them; they are simply not part of what is shared.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. BUILD PRODUCTS — 42 executables
#
# Reproducible from the pinned compiler with the one-line build command in
# each folder's README, whose sha256 is recorded there. A compiled binary is
# the *result* of reading a README, not part of the example.
#
# Generated by `list-artifacts.sh`, which finds them two ways and takes the
# union: the build command each folder documents, and every ELF under
# examples/. The list is explicit because the patterns do not work —
# "ignore every extension-less file" would also swallow badapple's
# `s40x30` and `t80`, which are data, and "ignore a file named after its own
# folder" is not expressible here.
#
# >>> build products, generated by list-artifacts.sh -- do not edit
/aoc/day01/day01
/aoc/day02/day02
/aoc/day03/day03
/aoc/day04/day04
/aoc/day05/day05
/aoc/day06/day06
/aoc/day07/day07
/aoc/day08/day08
/aoc/day09/day09
/aoc/day10/day10
/aoc/day11/day11
/aoc/day12/day12
/audio/audio
/badapple/badapple
/badapple/badapple160x60
/badapple/badapple80x30
/bignum/bignum
/bmpview/bmpview
/boids/boids
/crypto-chat/crypto_chat
/cube/cube
/donut/donut
/editor/editor
/fluid/fluid
/fractal/fractal
/hexview/hexview
/httpsrv/httpsrv
/indexer/indexer
/life/life
/mandelbrot/mandelbrot
/maze/maze
/maze/xcheck
/mud/mud
/perft/perft
/perft/queue_test
/raytracer/raytracer
/rest-client/rest-client
/roguelike/roguelike
/sandfall/sandfall
/toolkit-cli/toolkit_cli
/turtle/turtle
/wavefunction/wavefunction
/weather/weather
# <<< build products
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. VERIFICATION TOOLING — no Python, no shell
#
# 50 .py and 20 .sh, 531 KB: the gate itself, the corpus and determinism
# audits, the argument/network/invocation probes, and per-example pty
# captures, defect injectors, oracles and snapshots.
**/*.py
**/*.sh
__pycache__/
*.pyc
# Left behind by an investigation whose finding is filed in BUGS.md and
# reachable from bignum's README.
/bignum/repro/
# Scratch for re-verifying the ledgers; self-cleaning, so it only exists if a
# run was interrupted.
/_scratch/
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. THE REPO ROOT ALREADY IGNORES EXTENSION-LESS FILES HERE, AND IS MOSTLY
#    RIGHT — three exceptions are pulled back below
#
# `.gitignore` at the repository root ends with:
#
#     examples/**
#     !examples/**/
#     !examples/**/*.*
#
# with the comment "ignore files in examples without extensions (that means
# they are compiled binaries)". For 83 of the 86 extension-less files under
# examples/ that is exactly right: 44 compiled binaries and 39 `EXPECTED`
# goldens. **For three it is wrong**, and all three are data an example needs:
#
# (These counts were 61-of-64, 40 binaries and 21 goldens when first written,
# and every one of the three numbers was stale: the corpus grew by a folder and
# a half, so 21 goldens became 39.  A deny-list whose justification is a count
# rots silently -- nothing fails, the count is simply wrong -- so the numbers
# above are re-measured, and the three data files are still exactly three.)
#
#   badapple/s40x30   arithmetic-coded source video, 46 KB. Every one of
#                     badapple's three pipelines reads it.
#   badapple/t80      the low-resolution tile sheet, 15 KB, read by the 80x30
#                     pipeline.
#   indexer/data/tree/Makefile   part of the fixture tree indexer indexes.
#                     Its name appears twice in indexer's own EXPECTED.
#
# A file with no extension is not necessarily a binary; here it is three
# times data. Since a .gitignore nearer the file wins, these are re-included
# here rather than by editing the root file, which belongs to the compiler
# project and not to this campaign.
!/badapple/s40x30
!/badapple/t80
!/indexer/data/tree/Makefile
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. CHECK-ONLY ARTEFACTS
#
# Measured before ignoring: **no program reads any of these at run time.**
# Every mention of `EXPECTED`, `golden/` or `gate.conf` in a .y file is a
# comment; only the (now untracked) harnesses open them.
#
#   `**/` and not `*/`, and that is not a detail: `*/gate.conf` matched the
# 24 top-level folders and silently missed all twelve `aoc/dayNN/gate.conf`,
# because `*/` is exactly one directory level. It looked right -- every
# top-level gate.conf was gone -- and the count is what gave it away.
#
#   * `gate.conf` names the build, run and check commands. The build line is
#     the one thing that would be lost, and every README carries it in prose
#     anyway — it is how you build the example.
#   * `EXPECTED` is the byte-exact output the gate diffs against.
#   * `golden/` holds captured frames and check transcripts.
**/gate.conf
**/EXPECTED
**/golden/
#
# The real Advent of Code inputs. No day's source reads them and no gate
# references them; each day vendors the slice it needs into its own folder as
# `input.txt` / `example.txt`, and those vendored copies stay tracked. Five day
# READMEs name this directory as the shared copy they were vendored from.
/aoc/inputs/
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. WHAT IS TRACKED, AND WHY IT IS ENOUGH
#
#   *.y              the program, and its vendored toolkit copies
#   *.md             the README, which carries build, run and orientation
#   data/, *.txt     fixtures the programs open at run time -- bmp images,
#                    raw byte blobs, JSON and CSV samples, the per-day input
#   badapple/s40x30  frame source; badapple/t80  tile sheet (data, not code)
#   *.toml, *.lock   package manifests, for the ycc build/vendor examples
#
# A clone of this repository is therefore a set of programs you can read and
# run, and nothing else. That is the whole deliverable.
#
# Belt and braces, matching the repository root .gitignore:
*.o
a.out
*.pack
core
core.*